## When Codex is the Builder
*The reverse workflow: Codex writes the change, so the seats swap. **The method does not change —
only who sits where.** `core/GATES.md` § Routing is the authority on which family gates what; this
section names the concrete seats for THIS repo.*

- **Design gate → {{CROSS_FAMILY_LENS}}**, in design mode on the design-as-contract. Unchanged from
  the Claude-Builder row: both lanes point the cross-family lens at the design, never at a code
  changeset.
- **Code gate → the Claude lane.** Codex cannot gate itself, and the seat it cannot fill is the
  *code* gate. Bind the concrete reviewer invocation here: **{{CODEX_LANE_CODE_GATE}}**.
  ⚠ Bind a **concrete, current-generation model id and an explicit effort flag** — never a friendly
  alias. An alias may expand to a superseded generation or a credit-gated variant, and the gate then
  dies before reading the artifact with an entitlement error that is *not* a verdict
  (`core/GATES.md` § Model · effort matrix).
- **Cold panel → Codex**, and ⚠ **it MUST run through a guarded site** — `scripts/codex-gate.sh`
  (fail-closed `claude` shim on PATH + `PIL_BLOCK_CLAUDE_COMPANION=1`) or a hermetic `CODEX_HOME`.
  **This is a precondition of the routing above, not a nicety.** A Claude-companion Codex plugin
  hijacks review-shaped prompts in a bare `codex exec` into a real Claude review — non-empty and
  on-topic, so an output check passes it. An unguarded Codex panel plus a Claude code gate is
  **one family on the code with every box ticked** (`core/GATES.md` § Routing, § Gotchas / traps).
- **What does NOT change:** the tier, the ladder, the PM disposition contract, decorrelation, or the
  Owner's push-GO. The Builder-family cold panel is never substituted away; it is the spine, and the
  spine is family-relative.

## ⚠ Enforcement asymmetry — which controls bind which lane
*(This is the canonical statement of the PM-portability caveat. Since kit v2.1 the PreToolUse guards
register in the Codex lane too — but read the qualifier in that column as part of the value: a Codex
hook does not run until a human grants hook trust in an INTERACTIVE session, and an untrusted hook is
skipped SILENTLY. `node scripts/check-codex-hooks-armed.mjs` is what settles it. Every PreToolUse row
binds TOOL calls only — writes, and for `guard-brief-rung` cross-session sends as well: a file
rewritten by a plain shell command is invisible to them in either lane.)*

| Control | Claude lane | {{OTHER_LANE}} lane | Human by hand |
|---|---|---|---|
| `guard-cross-repo-writes` (writes outside repo blocked) | enforced | **enforced — only once hook trust is granted** | ❌ |
| `guard-lane-authoring` (declaration before a code write) | enforced | **enforced — only once hook trust is granted** | ❌ |
| `guard-gate-ladder` (surfaces the tier's ladder) | enforced (sensor) | **enforced (sensor) — only once hook trust is granted** | ❌ |
| `guard-brief-rung` (brief writes, cross-session sends, **and source writes while a repair is live**) | enforced | **enforced — only once hook trust is granted** | ❌ |
| `.githooks/pre-commit` (declaration at commit) | enforced | **enforced** | **enforced** |

`guard-brief-rung` is the row most likely to stop you, so read its shape here rather than meeting it
in a denial, and note that it has TWO halves with different trigger conditions.

- **Always live:** brief writes and cross-session sends need a fresh verification sidecar naming
  this session and this dispatch. No repair round is involved; this half binds from the moment the
  hook is registered.
- **Live only during a repair:** source writes. This half is dormant until a gate round is recorded
  — and dormancy is a state with an instructed exit, never a property. Once a round stands at NO-GO
  with disposition REMEDIATE and no ELIGIBLE recorded close, writes to the paths that repair claimed need a
  verified worker receipt from the writing session. Every other disposition holds nothing. A repair is ended by an
  **eligible close** — a pair of ledger rows whose conditions are defined in one place, beside
  `deriveRepairState`'s post-pass in `repair-dispatch-state.mjs`; read them there rather than from a
  summary. What matters at this level: the test compares SUPPLIED SESSION IDS, not actors, so a
  close records who it says it came from. It buys a legible row over the alternative it replaced,
  which was deleting the ledger. It is not an authorization boundary, and nothing here should be
  read as one.

It has one relief and it announces itself: in a tree that cannot hold a repair ledger at all, the
guard says it is blind and lets the source write through. A ledger it cannot READ is a different
answer — that denies.

**What binds every lane** is prose (this file + `AGENTS.md` + the required PM-disposition emission)
**plus the harness-agnostic `pre-commit` hook** — the one layer no harness routes around, because all
writers converge at the commit. A reader who assumes the PreToolUse guards are universal trusts a
control that is not running. **FM1:** `core.hooksPath` is local config, not tracked — a fresh clone
with it unset has the pre-commit control silently absent. The kit's `test:kit-controls` suite includes
a test that goes RED when `core.hooksPath` is unset, so an unconfigured clone is loud, not unguarded.

## Tool bindings
- **Cross-family lens / external gate:** {{GATE_TOOLING}} *(if you use the shipped Codex/Gemini
  runners, they need `codex` / `agy` at runtime — see `PORTABILITY.md`; if you gate by hand, say so
  here).*
- **Task-lane declaration — enforcement paths:** declaration `.claude/task-lane.json`; ledger
  `.claude/lane-ledger.jsonl` (gitignored); write-time guard `.claude/hooks/guard-lane-authoring.mjs`,
  commit-time floor `.githooks/pre-commit`. Routes: `in-thread` (with the tier) · `exempt` (with a
  ledgered reason). The retired `lane` route is REFUSED by both controls with an explicit
  `lane-retired` state (doctrine retired at kit v1.4.0, mechanism at v1.5.0 — history:
  `core/README.md` § Provenance). Rules: `core/MULTI_AGENT.md` § Task-lane declaration.
- **Repo-specific families** live in `.claude/kit.config.json` (`executedPathDirs`, `stateDocs`,
  `memoryDir`) — read by `guard-lane-authoring` and `check-doc-size`. A structurally corrupt file
  fails CLOSED in every control that reads one: it blocks a gated write, a **code-bearing** commit
  (a docs-only commit exits before the check), and `doc:size`. A legacy `laneRiskTokens` entry from a
  pre-v1.5 adopt is ignored whatever its shape — no control reads it.

